FRM.registerQuestions(48, [{"id":"972","page":19,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Var: 14; ES: 17"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Var: 14; ES: 16"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Var: 12; ES: 16"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Var: 12; ES: 24"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"After using of the historical simulation method, you have been provided with the following 30 ordered percentage returns for an asset: [-18, -16, -14, -12, -10, -9, -7, -7, -6, -6, -6, -5, -5, -4, -4, -4, -2, -1, 0, 0, 2, 3, 6, 12, 12, 13, 15, 15, 18, 28]"},{"type":"p","text":"The value-at-risk (VaR) and expected shortfall (ES), at 90% confidence level, respectively, are closest to:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR can be calculated as the [(1 − 0.9) × 30] = 3rd worst observation, which is -14 and hence the VaR is 14."},{"type":"p","text":"The ES is the arithmetic average of losses that are worse than the VaR. Thus,"},{"type":"p","text":"ES = (18 + 16) = 17"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"973","page":20,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Expected shortfall: 12; Value at Risk: 9.45"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Expected shortfall: 11.49; Value at Risk: 9.95"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Expected shortfall: 12; Value at Risk: 8.45"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Expected shortfall: 11.49; Value at Risk: 8.45"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Stuart Broad, a risk analyst working with Macquarie Bank compiles data of 100 simulated percentage returns of the bank’s assets: [-13.33, -12.25, -11.75, -10.66, -8.45, -7.13, -6.48, -5.29 ... 2.89, 3.56, 4.29, 5.38, 6.65, 7.89, 8.54, 9.64, 10.27, 12.28, 13.25]"},{"type":"p","text":"Using the data, he calculates the expected shortfall (ES) and the value at risk (VAR) of the bank’s assets at the 95 percent confidence level using the historical simulation method. What is the expected shortfall and the value at risk computed by Stuart Broad?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR can be calculated as the [(1 − 0.95) × 100] = 5th worst observation, which is -8.45 and hence the VaR is 8.45."},{"type":"p","text":"The ES is the arithmetic average of losses that are worse than the VaR. Thus,"},{"type":"pre","text":"ES  =  13.33  + 12.25     +  11.75  + 10=.6162"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"982","page":21,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 51.92 million"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 38.50 million"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 53.67 million"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 71.50 million"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"ANC National Bank handles a portfolio of assets amounting to USD 150 million. Antony Smith, a risk analyst, analyzes the portfolio and observes that the returns are independently identically normally distributed. The annual standard deviation of the portfolio is 0.55. What is the 20 day-Value at Risk at 95 percent confidence assuming 250 trading days in a year? Click here to see the standard normal table."}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"            V aR = Z × standard deviation × portfolio amount\n Annual VaR = 1.65 × 0.55 × 150,000,000 = 136, 125, 000\nVar (T days) = 1-day VaR × √T … … . formula\n   1-day VaR = Annual VaR = $8, 609,300\n                            √250\n 20-day VaR = (1-day VaR) × √20 = $38, 501,694"},{"type":"p","text":"It’s the standard deviation, σ, that determines where you start. If the σ given is annual, you’ll start by calculating the annual VaR and then use the square root of the time rule to come up with VaR for shorter periods. Similarly, if given the daily σ, compute the 1-day VaR and then convert that appropriately."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"2616","page":22,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$7,725.57"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$17,007"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$6,659.97"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$14,661.22"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Arthur Bell is the portfolio manager at FFF Investments. Recently, he bought 5,000 call options on stocks of one of the local growth-oriented oil refining companies that have never paid dividends. The strike price of the options was $50. The underlying stock is trading at $58 and has an annual volatility of return of 33%. Bell estimated the delta of these options to be 0.55. What is the approximate weekly (delta normal) 99% VaR of the position assuming 52 trading weeks in the year?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"In the delta-normal approach, we first calculate the VaR of the underlying. Then, we use the equation below to revalue the derivative by linear approximation (as the delta multiplied by the VaR of the underlying):"},{"type":"pre","text":"                            VaRderivative = Δ × VaRunderlying\nVaR of the underlying (stock) = σ × zcl% × market value"},{"type":"p","text":"But first, we have to convert the annual volatility, i.e., σ, into a weekly value:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Annual volatility = weekly volatility × √52"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus, weekly volatility = (33%) = 0.045763"},{"type":"pre","text":"                              √52\n     VaR of the underlying = 0.045763 ∗ 2.33 ∗ $58 = $6.18438\n           VaR of call option = 0.55 ∗ $6.18 = $3.40\nVaR of 5,000 call options = $3.40 × 5, 000 = $17,007"},{"type":"p","text":"Note: 2.33 is the standard normal deviate at a 99% confidence level."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"2619","page":23,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$3,457.99"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$5,791.77"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$4,524.05"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$6,388.50"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"After a significant increase in the volatility of shares of USY Pharma, Ross Grand, senior portfolio manager, decides to hedge the existing position by buying 300 at-the-money call options. Shares of USY are trading at $457 and the daily VaR of the underlying at 99% confidence is $42.59. What is the daily 99% VaR of the options position using the delta-normal method?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The key point here is that the call options are at the money, so we can assume delta = 0.5. (See the note at the end of the explanation if this is unclear.) According to the delta-norma method,"},{"type":"pre","text":"    VaR of an option = Delta × VaR of underlying\nVaR of the position = 0.5 ∗ $42.59 ∗ 300 options = $6, 388.50"},{"type":"p","text":"Note: At-the-money call options typically have a delta of 0.5, and the delta of out-of-the-money call options approaches 0 as expiration nears. The deeper in-the-money the call option, the closer the delta will be to 1, and the more the option will behave like the underlying asset. Since the delta of an option ranges between 0 and 1, an at-the-money option is right in between those two numbers. For put options, at-the-money put options typically have a delta of -0.5, and the delta of out-of-the-money call options approaches 0 as expiration nears. The deeper in-the-money put option, the closer the delta will be to -1."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"2623","page":23,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Futures on stocks, forwards on broad market indices, and plain vanilla European options on bonds."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Interest rate swaps, interest rate caps, and plain vanilla American options on stocks."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Futures on broad market indices."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Futures on stocks and swaptions."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Ashli More prepares a presentation to the management board on the application of derivatives for hedging risk. She struggles with the classification of linear and non-linear derivatives. Which of the following is an example of linear derivatives?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Futures on broad market indices are considered linear derivatives. This is because the payoff of a futures contract is linear in nature, meaning it changes proportionally with the changes in the underlying asset. In other words, if the price of the underlying asset increases, the value of the futures contract also increases proportionally, and vice versa. This characteristic makes futures contracts linear derivatives. Broad market indices are a common underlying asset for futures contracts, making futures on broad market indices a typical example of linear derivatives. Choice A is incorrect. While futures on stocks and forwards on broad market indices are examples of linear derivatives, plain vanilla European options on bonds are not. Options, whether they are European or American, call or put, are non-linear derivatives because their payoff is not directly proportional to the underlying asset's price movement. Choice B is incorrect. Interest rate swaps can be considered as linear derivatives as their value changes in proportion with the underlying interest rate. However, interest rate caps and plain vanilla American options on stocks are non-linear derivatives due to their asymmetrical payoff structure. Choice D is incorrect. Futures on stocks can be classified as linear derivatives but swaptions cannot be classified under the same category. Swaptions give the holder the right but not obligation to enter into a swap agreement in future which makes them a type of option and hence a non-linear derivative."}],"remember":["Linear derivatives have payoffs that change proportionally with the changes in the underlying asset.","Non-linear derivatives have payoffs that are not directly proportional to the underlying asset's price movement.","Futures contracts are considered linear derivatives because of their linear payoff structure.","Options, such as plain vanilla European or American options, are examples of non-linear derivatives due to their asymmetrical payoff structures.","Interest rate swaps can be classified as linear derivatives as their value changes in proportion with the underlying interest rate.","Swaptions are a type of option and hence classified as non-linear derivatives."]},{"id":"2625","page":25,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Stocks of ALPHA"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Stocks of APPA"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Bonds of APPA"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"It cannot be determined"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The investment division of a bank is considering an investment of $100,000,000 in one of the following:"},{"type":"pre","text":"stocks of ALPHA Plc\nstocks of APPA Corporation\n20-year bonds of APPA Corporation"},{"type":"p","text":"Although the department seeks the investment with the highest expected return, to comply with bank’s risk policies, the department cannot open a position with a daily 99% VaR higher than $6,000,000. The investment opportunities presented above have the following features:"},{"type":"pre","text":"Stocks of ALPHA  Expected Annual  Expected annual        Modified          Market Price\nStocks of APPA   Return           volatility of returns  Duration\nBonds of APPA                                                              $5 per stock\n                          20%                50%              −           $30 per stock\n                          13%                36%              −    99% per $100 nominal\n                           8%                10%             3.5"},{"type":"p","text":"Assuming zero daily returns and 252 trading days per year, which investment should the bank choose?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"pre","text":"VaR of ALPHA's stocks = 50% × 1 × 2.33 × $100, 000, 000 = $7, 338, 810.18\n                                                  √252\n   VaR of APPA's stocks = 36% × 1 × 2.33 × $100, 000, 000 = $5, 283, 943.33\n                                                  √252\n   VaR of APPA' s bond = 10% × 1 × 2.33 × $100, 000, 000 = $1, 467, 762.036\n                                                  √252"},{"type":"p","text":"Both the APPA's bond and APPA's stock have a VaR lower than $6, 000, 000, but the bank should choose to invest in APPA's stock since it has a higher return than APPA's bond."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"2626","page":26,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Manager Y."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Manager X."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Both managers will have the same VaR estimate."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Insufficient information to determine."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Two managers - X and Y - are looking to establish the 1-day VaR for a long position in an at-the-money call option on a non-dividend-paying stock with the following information: Current stock price: USD 100 Estimated annual stock return volatility: 15% Current Black-Scholes-Merton call option value: USD 4.80, Call option delta: 0.5 To compute VaR, manager X uses the delta-normal model, while manager Y opts for the Monte Carlo simulation method for full revaluation. Which manager will estimate a higher value for the 1-day 99% VaR?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Manager X, who uses the delta-normal model, will estimate a higher value for the 1-day 99% VaR. This is because options are nonlinear derivatives, meaning their value is related to the market price of the underlying variable in a convex, non-linear relationship. The payoff of such products varies not only with the value of the underlying, but also with other elements such as interest rates, volatility, and dividends. The option’s price function is convex with respect to the value of the underlying. For such a non-linear portfolio, the delta-normal model provides only a linear approximation which does not capture the positive effect of this curvature on the portfolio value. It understates the probability of high option values and overstates the probability of low option values. Therefore, for a long position in the call option, VaR and the expected shortfall under the delta normal model will be extremely high. On the other hand, for a short position in the call option, the VaR and the expected shortfall under the delta-normal model will be extremely low."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice A is incorrect. Manager Y, who uses the Monte Carlo simulation method for full revaluation, is not likely to estimate a higher value for the 1-day 99% VaR. This method takes into account all possible price paths and their associated probabilities, which includes both large and small changes in the option's value. Therefore, it tends to produce a more conservative (lower) VaR estimate compared to the delta-normal model."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. Both managers will not have the same VaR estimate because they are using different methods of calculation. The delta-normal model used by Manager X assumes that returns are normally distributed and only considers linear risks such as delta risk. On the other hand, Monte Carlo simulation used by Manager Y considers all types of risks including non-linear risks such as gamma and vega risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. There is sufficient information to determine which manager will likely estimate a higher value for 1-day 99% VaR based on their chosen methods of calculation."}],"remember":["Delta-normal model is a method used to estimate Value at Risk (VaR) by assuming that asset returns are normally distributed and only considering linear risks such as delta risk.","Monte Carlo simulation is a method used to estimate VaR by simulating all possible price paths and their associated probabilities, taking into account all types of risks including non-linear risks such as gamma and vega risk.","Options are nonlinear derivatives, meaning their value is related to the market price of the underlying variable in a convex, non-linear relationship.","The delta of an option measures the sensitivity of the option price to changes in the price of the underlying asset.","Expected shortfall is a risk measure that quantifies the expected loss in the tail of the distribution beyond the VaR level."]},{"id":"3305","page":27,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Unchanged."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR has increased by 0.01%."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR has increased by 0.08%."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR has increased by 0.11%."}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Bank A manages interest rate risk by monitoring the VaR using historical data. Bank A collects interest rate returns for 300 days and the data is sorted ascendingly. The lowest 10 interest rate returns are -4.2%, -4.0%, -3.8%, -3.2%, -3.0%, -2.5%, -2.3%, -2.2%, -2.0%, -1.7%. After 30 days, Bank A collects 30 more data points. However, none of these returns is less than -1.7%. What is the change in the 98% VaR as compared to the prior 30 days, assuming that all of the lowest 10 observations are still within the 300-day long historical window?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"In general, if there are n ordered observations, and a confidence level cl%, the cl% VaR is given by the [(1 − cl%) n ]th highest observation. This is the observation that separates the tail from the body of the distribution. For instance, if we have 1,000 observations and a confidence level of 95%, the 95% VaR is given by the (1 – 0.95)1,000 = 50st observation. There are 50 observations in the tail. Given this information, the 98% VaR, given that n = 300 is:"},{"type":"pre","text":"h"},{"type":"p","text":"98% VaR = [(1 - 0.98)300 th value = 6th highest observation Thus, VaR = -2.5% One important note about this method is that the length of the historical window is fixed, i.e., the oldest observations exit the window as new observations are made. In this case, however, 30 more days have now elapsed (meaning that we have 30 new observations) but there hasn't been a loss big enough to dislodge any of the worst 10 observations made, which we assume are still within the historical window of 300 observations. As such, the 98% VaR will still be the 6th highest observation which happens to be -2.5%. In short, the VaR remains unchanged."}],"remember":["VaR (Value at Risk) is a measure used to assess the potential loss in value of a risky asset or portfolio over a specified time period under normal market conditions.","Historical VaR uses historical data to estimate potential losses, assuming that past performance is indicative of future performance.","The confidence level in VaR represents the probability that the actual loss will not exceed the estimated VaR.","When calculating VaR, the observations are sorted in ascending order, and the VaR is determined by the nth highest observation based on the confidence level.","Changes in the historical window can impact the VaR calculation, as new data may replace older data in the calculation."]},{"id":"3307","page":29,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"-2.0%"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"-2.2%"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"-2.5%"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"-2.4%"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Bank A manages interest rate risk by monitoring the VaR calculated using historical data. Bank A collects interest rate returns for 300 days and the data is sorted ascendingly. The lowest 10 interest rate returns are -4.2%, -4.0%, -3.8%, -3.2%, -3.0%, -2.5%, -2.3%, -2.2%, -2.0%, -1.7%. Calculate the 98% VaR."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"In general, if there are n ordered observations, and a confidence level cl%, the cl% VaR is given by the [(1 – cl%)n ]th highest observation. This is the observation that separates the tail from the body of the distribution. In this case, we have 300 observations and a confidence level of 98%; the 98% VaR is given by the (1 – 0.98)300 = 6th observation, that's 2.5%"}],"remember":["VaR (Value at Risk) is a measure used to assess the potential loss in value of a risky asset or portfolio over a specified time period with a certain confidence level.","Historical VaR is calculated using historical data on asset returns and assumes that the past behavior of the asset will continue into the future.","The confidence level in VaR represents the probability that the actual loss will not exceed the VaR estimate.","Sorting the data in ascending order is a common step in calculating VaR as it helps in identifying the nth highest or lowest observation based on the confidence level.","VaR is a key risk management tool used by financial institutions to quantify and manage their exposure to market risk, credit risk, and operational risk."]},{"id":"3313","page":30,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"204 pesos"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"61.0 pesos"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"115.8 pesos"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"122.4 pesos"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An option on the INMEX (Mexican) stock index is struck on 2,522 pesos. The delta of the option is 0.6, and the annual volatility of the index is 25%. Using delta-normal assumptions, what is the 10-day VaR of the option at the 95% confidence level? Assume 260 days per year."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"As per the delta-normal method, the VaR of a derivative position is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"V aRDerivative = Delta × V aRUnderlying risk factor"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus, the first step is to determine the VaR of the underlying rsk factor. i.e., the index. But before that, notice that the question asks for the 10-day VaR, which means it is important that we work out the 1-day VaR and then use the square root of time rule:"},{"type":"pre","text":"    T-day volatility = 1-day Volatility × √T\n260-day volatility = 1-day volatility × √260"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus, 1-day volatility = 260-day volatility= 0.25 = 0.0155"},{"type":"pre","text":"√260  √260"},{"type":"p","text":"Hence,"},{"type":"pre","text":"1-day VaR of the underlying = 2, 522 × 0.0155 × 1.65 = 64.5 pesos\n                         10-day VaR = 1-day VaR × √(10) = 64.5 × √(10) = 204 pesos"},{"type":"p","text":"Finally,"},{"type":"pre","text":"V aRDerivative = Delta × V aRUnderlying risk factor\n                   = 0.6 × 204 = 122.4 pesos"},{"type":"p","text":"Note that this questions mixes concepts from the chapters Calculating and Applying VaR and Measuring and Monitoring Volatility."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3316","page":31,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$ 9.45"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$ 10.63"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$ 9.38"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$ 10.31"}]},"answer":"D","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Consider a non-linear portfolio that depends on a share price. Given that delta and gamma of the portfolio are 20 and 2.5, respectively. All else constant, what is the corresponding portfolio change to a stock price increase of $ 0.5?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Given the delta and gamma of a portfolio, the change in portfolio is given by :"},{"type":"pre","text":"ΔP  =  δ ΔS  +  1  γ(ΔS )2\n                2"},{"type":"p","text":"Where δ = Delta ΔS = Change in the share price γ = Gamma ΔP = Change in portfolio value. Thus,"},{"type":"pre","text":"ΔP  = 20 × 0.5 +                              1  × 2.5 × 0.52      = 10.3125"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3318","page":32,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 3.12"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 2.15"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 3.167"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"USD 4.52"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"You have been asked to estimate the VaR of GreenWood Corp. The company’s stock is currently trading at USD 308 and the stock has a daily volatility of 1.25%. Using the delta-normal method, the VaR at the 95% confidence level of a long position in an at-the-money put on this stock with a delta of -0.5 over a 1-day holding period is closest to:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"In the case of a linear derivative, VaR scales directly with the underlying risk factor, i.e.,"},{"type":"pre","text":"V aRlinear derivative = Δ × V aRunderlying risk factor\n                  V aR = Δ × 1.645 × σ × S0 = 0.5 × 1.645 × 0.0125 × 308\n                          = 3.1666"},{"type":"p","text":"Note: Just as the question dictates, this is an estimate: the accurate relationship is non-linear and we are actually omitting the curvature (option gamma). Also, we ignore the negative sign in the solution since a negative amount is implied. Note 2: This question mixes concepts from the chapters Calculating and Applying VaR and Measuring and Monitoring Volatility."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3320","page":33,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR = 30; Conditional VaR = 46."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR = 32; Conditional VaR = 47.6."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR = 36; Conditional VaR = 47.6."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR = 32; Conditional VaR = 46."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A market risk manager has gathered historical P&L data for his financial institution over the last 100 days. He intends to determine the VaR and the conditional VaR (CVaR) at 90% level of confidence using the historical simulation method. The worst 15 observation gathered (in million CAD) are: [-25, -27, -27, -28, -30, -32, -36, -38, -40, -43, -45, -52, -56, -58, -60]"},{"type":"p","text":"Calculate the VaR and the conditional VaR (CVaR)."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"We arrange the data in ascending order first:"},{"type":"p","text":"-60, -58, -56, -52, -45, -43, -40, -38, -36, -32, -30, -28, -27, -25"},{"type":"p","text":"VaR can be calculated as the [(1 - 0.9) × 100] = 10th worst observation, which is -32 and hence the VaR is 32."},{"type":"p","text":"Conditional VaR is simply another name for the expected shortfall. The ES is the arithmetic average of losses that are worse than the VaR. Thus,"},{"type":"p","text":"ES = (60 + 58 + 56 + 52 + 45 + 43 + 40 + 38 + 36)"},{"type":"pre","text":"                                               = 47.6\n9"},{"type":"p","text":"Note: Refer to table 2.3 of your chapter for proof."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3394","page":34,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$115,114"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$70,000"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$223,570"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$200,000"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A liquid asset K has a profit/loss distribution that’s independent and identically distributed. The position has a one-day VaR of $50,000 at the 95% level of confidence. Estimate the 10-day VaR of the same position at the 99% level of confidence."}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The first step should be to convert the one-day VaR at 95% to a one-day VaR at 99%. To do this, recall that:"},{"type":"p","text":"V aR95% uses the upper 5% point of the normal distribution, i.e., 1.645"},{"type":"p","text":"And the V aR99% uses the upper 1% point of the normal distribution, i.e., 2.326"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore, to convert the one-day VaR at 95% to a one-day VaR at 99%, we must multiply the"},{"type":"pre","text":"2.326"},{"type":"p","text":"former by a scale of 1.645"},{"type":"pre","text":"One-day  V aR99% =    2.326  ×   $  50,  000  = $70, 699\n                      1.645"},{"type":"p","text":"Next, recall that:"},{"type":"pre","text":"V aR (T days) = V aR (1 day) × √T"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore,"},{"type":"pre","text":"V aR10 days = $70, 699 × √10 = $223, 569.87"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"3395","page":35,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"1,260"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"1,653"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"1,190"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"1,609"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Peter McLeish is a risk analyst at Quantum Bank. After estimating the 99%, one-day VaR of the bank’s portfolio using historical simulation with 900 past days, he is concerned that the VaR is providing too little information on tail losses. He embarks on a deeper examination of simulation results. Sorting the simulated daily P&L from worst to best, he constructs the following table:"},{"type":"pre","text":"P & L Rank      1        2        3        4                         5\nP & L Rank  −2, 000  −1, 860  −1, 800  −1, 720                   −1, 630"},{"type":"p","text":"P & L Rank"},{"type":"pre","text":"    6        7        8        9                        10\n−1, 500  −1, 400  −1, 310  −1, 260                   −1, 190\n   11       12       13       13                        15\n−1, 110  −1, 050   −990     −820                      −750"},{"type":"p","text":"Determine the 99%, one-day expected shortfall of the portfolio:"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"When dealing with discrete data, let's first find the VaR. VaR can be calculated as the [(1 - 0.99)"},{"type":"p","text":"× 900] = 9th worst observation, which is -1,260 and hence the VaR is 1,260. The ES is the arithmetic average of losses that are worse than the VaR. Thus, ES = (2,000 + 1,860 + 1,800 + 1,720 + 1,630 + 1,500 + 1,400 + 1,310)/8 = 1,652.50"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4667","page":36,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"0.053, 0.1043"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0431, 0.0958"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0431, 0.1149"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"0.0431, 0.053"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The following are hypothetical ten worst returns for stock TGB from 120 days of data. Find the 1-day 95% VaR and expected shortfall, respectively. -15.72%, -10.92%, -6.50%, -3.56%, -6.90%, -2.50%, -5.30%, -4.31%.,-12.12%, -3.45%,"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"First, we rearrange starting with the worst day to the least bad day, as shown below: -15.72%, -12.12%, -10.92%, -6.90%, -6.50%, -5.30%, -4.31%, -3.56%, -3.45%, -2.50%. In this case, the VaR corresponds to the (5%×120)=6th worst day: -5.30%. This implies ES is equivalent to an average of the 5 worst returns:"},{"type":"pre","text":"      (−15.72% + −12.12% + −10.92% + −6.90% + −6.5%)\nES =     = −10.43%"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4668","page":37,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"85"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"-85"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"65"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"-65"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Consider a linear portfolio consisting of short positions in 50 shares, each worth USD 10, and a long position in 150 shares each worth USD 25. What is the relative portfolio change (in USD) of the portfolio if the price of all shares increases by 2%?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The change in a linear portfolio is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"ΔP = ∑ niΔSi\n                                           i"},{"type":"p","text":"Where ni: number of shares of stock i in the portfolio (negative for a short position) Si: Price of stock i So,"},{"type":"pre","text":"ΔP = [−50 × (0.02 × 10)] + [150 × (0.02 × 25)]\n      = −10 + 75 = 65"},{"type":"p","text":"So the change in the portfolio is an increase of USD 65."}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4670","page":38,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"604"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"505"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"662"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"645"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Given that the one-day standard deviation of a portfolio is 90, what is the 10-day VaR with a 99% confidence level according to the delta-normal model?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"According to the delta-normal model, we can assume that the mean change in risk factor is zero for a short period of time so that VaR is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"V aR = σP U"},{"type":"p","text":"Where σP : standard deviation of the portfolio U: point on the normal distribution where X is exceeded In this case, σP =90 and U =-2.326 and thus VaR is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"90 × −2.326 = −209.340"},{"type":"p","text":"We know that:"},{"type":"pre","text":"V aR (T , X) = √T × V aR (1,X)"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus the 10-day VaR is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"√10 × −209.340 = −661.99"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4671","page":39,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"640"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"759"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"650"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"749"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Given that the daily standard deviation of portfolio change is 90, what is the 10-day expected shortfall with a 99% confidence level according to the delta-normal model? Please click here to view the standard normal table"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"According to the delta-normal model, for a short period of time, we can assume that the mean change in risk factor is zero so that VaR is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"ES          σP  ⎛        e−U22                       ⎞\n        =       ⎜  (1  −    X)   √2π                 ⎟\n                ⎝                                    ⎠"},{"type":"p","text":"Where σP : standard deviation of the portfolio U: point on the normal distribution where X is exceeded In this case, σP =90 and U =2.326 and thus ES is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"           ⎛           (2.326)  2  ⎞\n                           2\n           ⎜⎜      e−           √2π⎠⎟⎟\nES  =  90  ⎝                            =  240.0635\n               (1  −  0.99)"},{"type":"p","text":"But we know that:"},{"type":"pre","text":"ES (T , X) = √T × ES (1, X)"},{"type":"p","text":"Thus the 10-day ES is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"√10 × 240.06 = 759.1473 ≈ 759"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4675","page":39,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR would be the fifth-worst loss."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR would be the average of first, second, third, and fourth-worst losses."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR would be the sixth-worst loss."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR would be the average of the fifth and sixth loss."}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A risk manager conducts 500 historical simulations to calculate one day, 99% VaR. Which of the following describes the value of VaR in this case?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Value at Risk (VaR) is a statistical technique used to measure and quantify the level of financial risk within a firm or investment portfolio over a specific time frame. In this case, the risk manager is calculating the one-day, 99% VaR using 500 historical simulations. This means that the VaR would be the fifth-worst loss. This is because when the losses are ordered in descending order, the fifth-worst loss (which is equivalent to 1% of 500) would represent the VaR. This value represents the maximum loss that the financial institution could experience with a 99% confidence level over a one-day period."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice B is incorrect. The Value at Risk (VaR) is not calculated as the average of the first, second, third, and fourth-worst losses. VaR represents a measure of risk that quantifies potential losses within a certain confidence level over a specific time horizon. In this case, with 500 simulations and a 99% confidence level, the VaR would be represented by the fifth-worst loss out of all simulations."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice C is incorrect. The VaR would not be represented by the sixth-worst loss in this scenario. As mentioned above, given 500 simulations and a 99% confidence level, it's the fifth-worst loss that represents the one-day VaR for this financial institution."},{"type":"p","text":"Choice D is incorrect. The calculation of VaR does not involve averaging any set of worst losses - in this case, it's not an average of fifth and sixth worst losses. It's simply represented by one single data point - here being the fifth-worst loss from all simulations."}],"remember":["VaR (Value at Risk) is a measure of the potential loss on an investment or portfolio over a specific time period for a given confidence interval.","Historical simulation is a method of calculating VaR by using historical data to simulate potential future outcomes.","The confidence level in VaR represents the probability that actual losses will not exceed the VaR estimate.","When calculating VaR using historical simulations, the VaR is typically based on the nth-worst loss, where n is determined by the confidence level and number of simulations.","VaR is a key tool used by risk managers to assess and manage the level of risk in a financial institution or investment portfolio."]},{"id":"4676","page":41,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"80.54"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"65.25"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"79.06"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"78.54"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"The 100-day 99% VaR for a portfolio is 50. What is the corresponding 250-day 99% VaR?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Using the formula"},{"type":"pre","text":"V aR (T , X) = √T × V aR (1, X)\n⇒ V aR (1, X) =  V aR (T , X)\n                      √T"},{"type":"p","text":"We, therefore, need first to calculate the one-day 99% VaR and then translate it to 250-day VaR"},{"type":"pre","text":"V  aR  (250,  99%)  =  √250  ×  [  50    ]  =  79.06\n                                   √100"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4677","page":42,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"30"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"50"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"40"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"20"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A risk manager realizes over a period of one month, a portfolio he manages increased by USD 5 when the stock price increased by USD 0.1.What is the value of portfolio delta?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Greek letter deltas (δ) is defined as:"},{"type":"pre","text":"      ΔP\nδ = ΔS"},{"type":"p","text":"Where ΔS is a small change in risk factors such as stock price and ΔP the corresponding change in the portfolio value. So,"},{"type":"pre","text":"        5\nδ = 0.1 = 50"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4678","page":43,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"66.78"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"56.78"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"19.82"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"94.56"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A risk manager estimates 10-day 95% VaR using the delta-model to be USD 50. What is the corresponding one-day expected shortfall at a 95% confidence level? Please click here to view the standard normal table"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Since we are dealing with a short time period, then:"},{"type":"pre","text":"       V aR = σP U\nES     σP  ⎛      e−U22       ⎞\n    =      ⎜  (1  −  X)  √2π  ⎟\n           ⎝                  ⎠"},{"type":"p","text":"X: confidence level U: point on the normal distribution where X is exceeded σP :standard deviation of the portfolio change From the question, we are given that the 10-day 95% VaR is 50, and thus one-day VaR is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":" 50\n√10 = 15.81"},{"type":"p","text":"We have X=95%, then U = 1.645."},{"type":"pre","text":"⇒ 15.81 = σP × 1.645\n                 15.81\n        σP = 1.645= 9.612"},{"type":"p","text":"Therefore, the ES is given by:"},{"type":"pre","text":"           ⎛                    e−  U2      ⎞             ⎛      e−  1.6452  ⎞\n           ⎜                         2      ⎟             ⎜             2    ⎟\n           ⎝ (1                             ⎠             ⎝ (1\nES  =  σP                       − X) √2π       =  9.612          − 0.95) √2π⎠   =  19.8220"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"4679","page":44,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Calculation of VaR and ES should concentrate on what happens in extreme market conditions."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Calculation of VaR and ES should concentrate on what happens in normal market conditions."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Calculation of VaR and ES should concentrate on what happens in both normal and extreme market conditions."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Calculation of VaR and ES should ignore changes in correlation during high market volatility."}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"In the context of financial risk management, the phenomenon of correlation breakdown is observed during periods of high market volatility, where correlations deviate from their usual patterns seen under normal market conditions. Given this scenario, what should be the primary focus when calculating Value at Risk (VaR) and Expected Shortfall (ES), two key measures used in risk management?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Correlations in a high volatility period are quite different from those of normal market conditions. When calculating VaR or ES, risk managers should determine what will happen in extreme market conditions. Therefore risk managers should try to determine correlations in extreme conditions rather than those in normal market conditions."},{"type":"p","text":"Opions B and C are incorrect since correlation breakdown implies that extreme market conditions should be considered rather than normal market conditions."},{"type":"p","text":"Opions D is incorrect because ignoring changes in correlation during periods of high volatility would lead to inaccurate risk estimates. Correlation breakdowns can significantly impact the joint distribution of asset returns and, consequently, the calculation of risk measures like VaR and ES."}],"remember":["Correlation breakdown can lead to underestimation of portfolio risk during periods of high market volatility.","During times of crisis, assets that were previously thought to be uncorrelated may become highly correlated.","Calculating VaR and ES using historical data may not capture the true risk during periods of correlation breakdown.","Stress testing is a useful tool to assess the impact of correlation breakdown on portfolio risk.","Using copulas can help model the joint distribution of asset returns more accurately, especially during times of correlation breakdown."]},{"id":"4681","page":45,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"16"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"17"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"18"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"17.5"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"You have been given the following 30 ordered percentage returns of an asset: [-18,-16,-14,-12,-10,-9,-7,-7,-6,-6,-6,-5,-5,-4,-4,-4,-2,-1,0,0,2,3,6,12,12,13,15,15,18,28] What is the expected shortfall at a 90% confidence level?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"To locate the 10%, we take the third-worst return (=(1-0.90)*30), which is -14. However, recall that VaR need not be represented as a negative. The ES is the arithmetic average of losses beyond 90%. Thus,"},{"type":"pre","text":"         18 + 16\nES = 2 = 17"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"6223","page":46,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"42.9"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"32.9"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"33"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"22.9"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A portfolio manager is analyzing the risk of a portfolio consisting of equities. The manager wants to determine the VaR for the portfolio over a one-year period at a 95% confidence level. The loss distribution is normal with a mean of 10 and a standard deviation of 20. What is the one-year VaR for the portfolio using the delta normal approach?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The VaR at a 95% confidence level can be calculated using the formula:"},{"type":"pre","text":"VaR = μ + Z σ"},{"type":"p","text":"where Z is the critical value from the standard normal distribution (Z=1.645 for 95% confidence), μ is the mean, and σ is the standard deviation. For this portfolio,"},{"type":"pre","text":"VaR = 10 + 1.645 × 20 = 10 + 32.9 = 42.9"}],"remember":[]},{"id":"6228","page":47,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"The portfolio is linear because equity positions dominate."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"The portfolio is nonlinear because it includes derivative structures."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"The portfolio is linear concerning the equity positions but nonlinear concerning the derivatives."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"The portfolio is neither linear nor nonlinear, due to its mixed nature."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A portfolio manager wants to classify a portfolio containing various financial instruments to understand its risk behavior better. The portfolio consists of a combination of straightforward equity positions and complicated derivative structures such as options on those equities. Which of the following correctly describes this portfolio?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The portfolio is linear concerning the equity positions because the change in value of the equity shares is directly proportional to changes in the share prices. However, it is nonlinear concerning the derivatives such as options, whose payoff structures vary non-linearly with changes in the underlying asset prices. A is incorrect because while the equity positions are linear, the inclusion of derivative structures, which are nonlinear, means the portfolio as a whole cannot be classified as entirely linear. B is incorrect because it overlooks the linear behavior of the equity positions, focusing only on the complex derivatives. D is incorrect because we can classify the portfolio by breaking it down into its constituent components: linear for equities and nonlinear for derivatives."}],"remember":["Linear instruments, like equities, have a direct, proportional relationship between changes in the instrument's value and changes in the underlying asset price.","Nonlinear instruments, such as options, have payoffs that change in a non-proportional manner relative to changes in the underlying asset's price. Their value can change at an accelerating or decelerating rate."]},{"id":"6229","page":48,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR is better because it provides a clear threshold for potential losses."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR provides a more conservative estimate of risk compared to ES."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"ES provides a better measure of extreme losses beyond the VaR threshold."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"ES is less reliable because it averages losses within the confidence interval."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A portfolio manager is evaluating the risk measures Value at Risk (VaR) and Expected Shortfall (ES) for a portfolio with a normally distributed return. Which of the following statements most accurately describes why ES might be preferred over VaR for comprehensive risk management?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"ES is preferred because it considers the average of the worst losses beyond the VaR threshold. A is incorrect. VaR provides a threshold but does not account for the magnitude of losses beyond this threshold. B is incorrect. ES considers the average of the worst losses beyond the VaR threshold, which provides a more comprehensive and often more conservative estimate of extreme risk. D is incorrect. ES focuses on tail losses beyond the VaR threshold, not just within the confidence interval."}],"remember":["Expected Shortfall (ES), also known as Conditional Value at Risk (CVaR), measures the average loss given that the loss exceeds the VaR threshold, providing a more comprehensive view of extreme risks.","VaR provides a threshold for potential losses at a specific confidence level but does not give information about the severity of losses beyond this threshold."]},{"id":"6231","page":49,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"It cannot capture nonlinear relationships between risk factors."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"It assumes past market behaviors will repeat themselves in the future."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"It assumes that asset returns are normally distributed."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"It is not suitable for portfolios with a large number of assets."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A portfolio manager is trying to understand the benefits and limitations of the historical simulation approach to computing Value at Risk (VaR). Which of the following statements correctly identifies a limitation of the historical simulation approach?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The historical simulation approach assumes that past market behaviors are indicative of future risks, which may not always hold true, especially during periods of market stress or structural changes. This reliance on historical data can sometimes lead to inaccurate risk predictions if market conditions change significantly. A is incorrect. The historical simulation approach can capture nonlinear relationships because it uses actual historical returns. C is incorrect. The historical simulation does not assume that asset returns are normally distributed; it uses the actual distribution of historical returns. D is incorrect. The approach can be applied to portfolios with a large number of assets, though it might become computationally intensive."}],"remember":["The historical simulation approach is non-parametric, meaning it does not assume any specific distribution for asset returns. It directly uses historical data to compute VaR, making it flexible in terms of distribution assumptions.","Method is most effective when historical data encompasses a range of market conditions similar to what might be expected in the future."]},{"id":"6232","page":50,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"By taking the average of the top 1% best-performing days."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"By identifying the 99th percentile loss and using it as the ES."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"By taking the average of the losses beyond the 99th percentile VaR."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"By using the Monte Carlo method to simulate the tail losses."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"When using the historical simulation approach to estimate the Expected Shortfall (ES) for a portfolio at a 99% confidence level, how is the ES calculated from the historical loss distribution?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Expected Shortfall (ES) at a 99% confidence level using the historical simulation approach involves calculating the average of the losses that occur beyond the 99th percentile VaR (i.e., the tail losses). This provides a more comprehensive measure of tail risk as compared to VaR. A is incorrect because ES focuses on the worst-performing days, not the best-performing ones. B is incorrect because while the 99th percentile loss is related to VaR, ES requires averaging the losses beyond this point. D is incorrect because the historical simulation approach directly uses historical data, not the Monte Carlo simulation for tail losses."}],"remember":["ES is calculated as the average of losses beyond the VaR threshold, particularly at extreme percentiles like 99%.","Historical simulation provides a direct method to estimate ES using actual historical loss data, offering good insights into tail risk.","Proper ranking and accurate identification of losses beyond the desired confidence level are crucial for calculating ES using historical simulation."]},{"id":"6233","page":51,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$10"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$16.45"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$23.29"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$7.23"}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A financial analyst uses the delta-normal approach to calculate the 1-day VaR of a portfolio composed of non-linear derivatives like options. The portfolio's delta is 0.5, the underlying's price volatility is 20%, and the initial value of the underlying is $100. Assuming a 95% confidence level, what is the 1-day VaR of this portfolio?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The delta-normal approach uses the delta of the portfolio to approximate the change in portfolio value. The 1-day VaR is calculated using the formula: VaR = ΔP = δΔS. Given δ = 0.5, the underlying's standard deviation (σ) = 0.2 × $100 = $20. At a 95% confidence level, the z-score equals -1.645. Thus, VaR = 0.5 × 20 × 1.645 = $16.45."}],"remember":["The delta-normal approach relies on approximating changes in portfolio value using the portfolio's delta and underlying asset's volatility.","VaR at a given confidence level often uses the corresponding z-score from the normal distribution.","This approach is straightforward but works best for smaller changes in value and less effectively for non-linear portfolios."]},{"id":"6234","page":52,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Delta measures the time decay of an option's value."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Delta indicates the curvature of the portfolio's value."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Delta represents the rate of change of the portfolio's value."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Delta measures the sensitivity of the option's price to volatility."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A financial analyst is assessing the sensitivity of a portfolio containing several options to changes in the price of the underlying asset. The analyst is particularly interested in understanding how the portfolio's value will change with small movements in the underlying asset price. Which of the following correctly describes the role of delta in this context?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Delta quantifies how much the value of an option or portfolio of options is expected to change with a one-unit change in the price of the underlying asset. It is a first-order measure of sensitivity to price movements. A is incorrect because delta measures price sensitivity, not time decay. Time decay is measured by theta. B is incorrect because delta measures linear sensitivity, while curvature (second-order sensitivity) is measured by gamma. D is incorrect because delta does not measure sensitivity to volatility changes; that measure is called vega."}],"remember":["Delta represents the rate of change of an option's or portfolio's value with respect to changes in the price of the underlying asset. It measures the sensitivity of the option's value to small movements in the underlying asset's price.","Delta is a first-order risk measure, indicating the linear sensitivity of the portfolio's value. For example, a delta of 0.5 means that for every $1 increase in the underlying asset's price, the option's value will increase by $0.50.","Time decay of an option's value is measured by theta, not delta. Theta quantifies how much the value of an option decreases as it approaches its expiration date.","Curvature or the rate of change of delta itself is measured by gamma, which is a second-order risk measure. Sensitivity to changes in volatility is measured by vega, not delta."]},{"id":"6236","page":53,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$480,000"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$333,333"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$500,000"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$600,000"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A risk manager is tasked with calculating the 1-day 99% VaR for a portfolio consisting solely of linear derivatives using a sensitivity parameter Δ derived from underlying asset prices. The sensitivity parameter Δ is 1.2, and the 1-day 99% underlying factor VaR is $400,000. What is the 1-day 99% VaR for the portfolio?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"For linear derivatives, the VaR can be calculated using the formula:"},{"type":"pre","text":"VaRlinear derivative = Δ × VaRunderlying factor"},{"type":"p","text":"Given Δ = 1.2 and the underlying factor VaR = $400, 000, the portfolio VaR = 1.2 × 400, 000 = $480, 000."}],"remember":["For linear derivatives, the VaR is calculated using the sensitivity parameter Δ multiplied by the underlying factor VaR.","The formula effectively measures the potential change in value of a linear derivative with proportional changes in the underlying factor."]},{"id":"6237","page":54,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"$200,000"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"$300,000"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"$187,500"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"$125,000"}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A trader managing a portfolio with several linear-term government bonds seeks to calculate the 1-day 95% VaR. The portfolio has a sensitivity Δ of 0.8 to interest rate changes, and the 1-day 95% underlying interest rate VaR is estimated to be $250,000. What is the portfolio VaR under these conditions?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The VaR for a portfolio of linear derivatives is calculated using the formula"},{"type":"pre","text":"VaR for linear derivative = Δ × VaR of the underlying factor"},{"type":"p","text":"Given that Δ = 0.8 and the interest rate VaR = $250, 000, the portfolio VaR = 0.8 × 250, 000 = $200, 000."}],"remember":["The sensitivity parameter Δ reflects how much the portfolio value changes with small changes in the underlying factor.","For interest rate sensitive portfolios, understanding the correct application of Δ ensures accurate risk assessments."]},{"id":"6238","page":55,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"It overestimates the impact of skewness and kurtosis in the underlying asset returns."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"It ignores normal distribution assumptions."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"It inadequately models the higher-order risks associated with non-linear derivatives."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"It assumes perfectly correlated risk factors."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Why might the delta-normal approach underestimate the risk for portfolios with significant non-linear derivative positions?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The delta-normal approach may underestimate risk for significant non-linear derivatives because it inadequately models higher-order risks like gamma and vega, which are crucial for capturing the curvature and volatility sensitivity in derivatives’ values. A is incorrect because the delta-normal approach does not overestimate impact-associated higher moments like skewness and kurtosis. B is incorrect because it does not ignore normal distribution assumptions; rather, it relies on them, which is part of its limitation. D is incorrect because the assumption of perfectly correlated risk factors typically reduces diversification benefits, not generally increasing the overall risk estimate."}],"remember":["Non-linear derivatives like options have higher-order risks, such as gamma and vega; the delta-normal method inadequately accounts for these.","Assuming normally distributed returns and linear risk factors often leads to underestimation of extreme and tail risks.","Diversification impacts and risk factor correlations are treated simplistically in the basic delta-normal approach."]},{"id":"6239","page":55,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Historical VaR"}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Parametric VaR"}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Monte Carlo VaR"}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Standard Deviation"}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"A risk manager is evaluating the effectiveness of different risk measures for a portfolio that includes equities, bonds, and derivatives. The manager wants to ensure that the chosen risk measure can adequately handle non-linear instruments and capture extreme market movements. Which of the following risk measures would be most appropriate in this scenario?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Monte Carlo VaR is particularly effective for portfolios that include non-linear instruments like derivatives and for capturing extreme market movements. The method uses simulations to model a wide range of possible outcomes, allowing it to handle the complexities and non-linearities inherent in such portfolios. A is incorrect because while Historical VaR uses real historical data and can capture some extreme movements, it may not fully account for non-linear instruments as effectively as Monte Carlo simulations. B is incorrect because Parametric VaR assumes a normal distribution of returns and may not adequately capture the non-linearities and tail risks associated with derivatives. D is incorrect because Standard Deviation is a measure of volatility, not risk, and does not capture the tail risks or non-linear characteristics of the portfolio's instruments."}],"remember":["Monte Carlo VaR is a simulation-based method that models a wide range of possible outcomes, making it highly effective for portfolios with non-linear instruments like derivatives.","By generating numerous random price paths for the portfolio's assets, Monte Carlo VaR can capture extreme market movements and provide a more accurate risk assessment under various scenarios.","This approach does not rely on the assumption of normally distributed returns, which is crucial for accurately measuring the risks associated with derivatives and other complex financial instruments.","Monte Carlo VaR can be computationally intensive but offers a flexible and robust framework for evaluating the risk of portfolios with diverse asset classes and non-linear exposures."]},{"id":"6240","page":57,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"It cannot handle the complexity of assets with non-linear payoff structures."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"The results can be very sensitive to the input assumptions and require extensive computational resources."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"It assumes normally distributed returns, which can lead to underestimated risks."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"It fails to provide insights into the underlying risk factors affecting the portfolio."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An investment firm uses Monte Carlo simulation to estimate the 1-day 99% VaR and ES for their asset portfolio. What is a notable weakness of this method?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"A notable weakness of the Monte Carlo simulation method is its sensitivity to input assumptions and the extensive computational resources required. The accuracy of the output depends strongly on the quality of the input assumptions, and simulations can be time-consuming and resource-intensive. A is incorrect because Monte Carlo simulation is well-suited to handling non-linear payoff structures. C is incorrect because Monte Carlo simulations do not necessarily assume normally distributed returns; they can accommodate various distribution types. D is incorrect because the method can provide insights into risk factors, though this depends on the design of the simulations."}],"remember":["The accuracy of Monte Carlo simulation results hinges on the quality of the input assumptions used in the model.","The method requires significant computational power, making it less practical for quick calculations but essential for detailed risk analysis.","Model risk and implementation risk can affect the reliability of the simulation outputs, necessitating careful scenario planning and validation."]},{"id":"6241","page":58,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"By providing a single-point estimate of the median loss in the portfolio."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"By generating a distribution of potential portfolio values and calculating the average of the losses beyond the VaR threshold."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"By relying on historical data to estimate the worst possible portfolio loss."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"By assuming that the portfolio returns are normally distributed and calculating ES directly from this assumption."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"When applying the Monte Carlo simulation method, a risk manager runs 10,000 simulations for a portfolio consisting of equity and option positions. How does the Monte Carlo simulation approach help in calculating the Expected Shortfall (ES) for such a complex portfolio?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"The Monte Carlo simulation approach generates a distribution of potential portfolio values and calculates the Expected Shortfall (ES) by taking the average of the losses that occur beyond the VaR threshold. This method provides a detailed view of tail risk for complex portfolios that include non-linear payoff structures. A is incorrect because ES is not a single-point estimate of the median loss; it is an average of tail losses. C is incorrect because Monte Carlo simulations rely on hypothetical scenarios rather than just historical data to estimate portfolio losses. D is incorrect because Monte Carlo simulations can be used with various assumed distributions and are not limited to normally distributed returns."}],"remember":["Monte Carlo simulation helps calculate Expected Shortfall (ES) by generating a distribution of potential portfolio values through numerous random scenarios. For example, running 10,000 simulations provides a comprehensive set of possible outcomes for the portfolio.","To calculate ES, identify the portfolio's Value at Risk (VaR) at the desired confidence level (e.g., 95%). Then, focus on the worst losses beyond this VaR threshold.","ES is computed by averaging the losses that occur beyond the VaR threshold, providing a more detailed understanding of tail risk and potential extreme losses for complex portfolios with non-linear payoff structures."]},{"id":"6242","page":59,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"It is known as systematic risk increase and implies higher diversification benefits."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"It is known as correlation breakdown and implies that standard risk models may underestimate risk during stress periods."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"It is known as volatility clustering and implies a steady risk profile across different periods."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"It is known as market normalization and implies that correlations become negligible."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"During the 2007-2008 financial crisis, many financial institutions observed a significant change in the correlations between asset returns under stressed market conditions. What is this phenomenon known as, and what implication does it have for VaR or ES analysis?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"This phenomenon is known as correlation breakdown. During stressed market conditions, asset return correlations often increase. This implies that standard risk models, which typically assume stable correlations, may underestimate risk during stress periods, making VaR and ES calculations less reliable. A is incorrect because correlation breakdown relates to increased correlations, which reduces diversification benefits, not increases them. C is incorrect because volatility clustering pertains to periods of high volatility following one another but does not directly address changing correlations. D is incorrect because correlation breakdown implies increased correlations, not negligible correlations, during market stress."}],"remember":["Correlation breakdown refers to the phenomenon where asset correlations increase during stressed market conditions, affecting risk models.","This breakdown leads to underestimating risk in VaR and ES models if not accounted for correctly.","Adjustments for time-varying correlations are necessary to improve risk estimation accuracy under stress scenarios."]},{"id":"6243","page":60,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Traditional models may overestimate diversification benefits and underestimate potential losses."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Traditional models become impossible to apply as correlations stabilize."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR will underestimate potential gains because it assumes that asset returns will move in the same direction."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"ES becomes less relevant because tail risk becomes of less importance."}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An asset manager observes a significant increase in the correlations between various assets during a period of market stress. What challenges does this correlation breakdown present when using traditional VaR or ES models?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"During a period of market stress, correlation breakdown means that correlations between various assets increase, reducing diversification benefits. Traditional VaR and ES models that assume stable correlations tend to overestimate diversification and underestimate potential losses, making them less reliable in such scenarios. B is incorrect because increased correlations make model application more complex, not simpler. C is incorrect because VaR models are designed to estimate potential losses, not gains. Increased correlations usually lead to underestimation of losses due to reduced diversification benefits. D is incorrect because, in reality, tail risk becomes more critical, and ES becomes even more relevant during periods of increased asset correlations and market stress."}],"remember":["Correlation breakdown reduces diversification benefits, making traditional VaR and ES models underestimate risk during market stress.","Accurate risk modeling under stress conditions requires dynamically adjusting for increased asset correlations."]},{"id":"6244","page":61,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"Continue using the same correlation assumptions to keep the model simple and efficient."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"Incorporate stressed correlations estimated from past periods of high volatility."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"Eliminate correlations from the model as they become less relevant."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Assume zero correlations to cover the worst-case scenario."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"How should a financial institution adjust its risk modeling approach when dealing with correlation breakdown during periods of high volatility?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"To adjust for correlation breakdown during periods of high volatility, a financial institution should incorporate stressed correlations estimated from historical periods of high volatility. This helps in better capturing the risk dynamics and providing more accurate VaR and ES estimates under stress scenarios. A is incorrect because continuing with the same correlation assumptions makes the model inaccurate under stress conditions. C is incorrect because eliminating correlations altogether disregards the relationship between assets, oversimplifying the model and losing information. D is incorrect because assuming zero correlations oversimplifies the risk profile and misrepresents potential diversification benefits."}],"remember":["Incorporating stressed correlations from past periods of high volatility improves the accuracy of risk models during periods of correlation breakdown.","Dynamic adjustment of correlation assumptions during stress conditions is crucial for robust VaR and ES estimation."]},{"id":"6245","page":62,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"The increased correlations will lead to overestimating possible losses."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"The increased correlations will reduce the diversification benefits, potentially leading to underestimating the true risk."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"The calculation will remain unaffected, as standard models are robust to correlation changes."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"The increased correlations will necessitate the use of a historical simulation approach exclusively."}]},"answer":"B","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"During a period of financial stress, a risk analyst observes that the correlations between the asset classes in their portfolio have significantly increased. What should be the analyst’s primary concern regarding the calculation of VaR and ES?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"Increased correlations during financial stress periods reduce diversification benefits, potentially leading to an underestimation of the true risk if the VaR and ES models don’t account for changing correlations adequately. A is incorrect because increased correlations during stress periods typically lead to underestimating rather than overestimating risk. C is incorrect because standard VaR and ES models assuming constant correlations are affected significantly by changes in correlations. D is incorrect because while historical simulation can be used, it’s not the exclusive method; adjustments for correlations can be made in other models too."}],"remember":["High correlations reduce diversification effects, leading to potential underestimation of risk in VaR and ES models.","Risk models should dynamically adjust correlations, especially under stress scenarios, to accurately reflect risk."]},{"id":"6246","page":63,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR and ES will likely decrease, and the firm should continue using the same correlation matrix."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR and ES will remain unchanged, and the firm should focus on other risk measures."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR and ES will likely increase due to higher correlations, and the firm should adjust the models to account for this change."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR and ES will become irrelevant, and the firm should stop using them temporarily."}]},"answer":"C","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"An investment firm notices that the correlations between its portfolio's asset returns have risen significantly due to a market shock. What impact does this have on the firm’s calculated VaR and ES, and how should the firm address this issue?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"When correlations between asset returns rise significantly due to a market shock, VaR and ES are likely to increase because higher correlations reduce diversification benefits. The firm should address this issue by adjusting its models to account for the higher correlations, ensuring that risk measures accurately reflect the new risk environment. A is incorrect because VaR and ES are more likely to increase, not decrease, with rising correlations. B is incorrect because VaR and ES are significantly impacted by changes in correlations and should not be ignored. D is incorrect because VaR and ES remain relevant even under changed conditions; they need adjustment to remain accurate."}],"remember":["Rising correlations during market shocks increase VaR and ES by reducing diversification benefits, reflecting higher overall risk.","Investment firms should adjust risk models to dynamically reflect changing correlations to maintain accurate risk assessments."]},{"id":"6247","page":64,"options":{"A":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR identifies the maximum loss at a certain confidence level, while WCS details the extreme loss if the worst-case event occurs."}],"B":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR provides a detailed loss distribution, while WCS only looks at probable losses within a confidence interval."}],"C":[{"type":"p","text":"WCS measures expected losses accurately within a standard deviation, while VaR focuses on tail risks."}],"D":[{"type":"p","text":"Both methods provide the same level of detail regarding the distribution of losses in the tail."}]},"answer":"A","stem":[{"type":"p","text":"Which of the following highlights a key difference between worst-case scenario analysis and Value at Risk (VaR) analysis in the context of risk management?"}],"explanation":[{"type":"p","text":"VaR is focused on predicting the maximum loss at a certain confidence level, typically neglecting how severe losses can get beyond this level. On the other hand, worst-case scenario (WCS) analysis goes beyond to detail the potential extremes of losses, assuming an unfavorable event causing worst outcomes. B is incorrect because VaR does not provide a full loss distribution, and WCS doesn't limit itself to typical confidence intervals but provides insights into extreme scenarios. C is incorrect because WCS focuses on tail-end losses beyond standard deviations, while VaR provides probable losses within confidence intervals. D is incorrect because WCS and VaR do not provide an equivalent level of detail concerning tail loss distributions; WCS offers more granularity in the extreme loss spectrum."}],"remember":["VaR specifies maximum losses at a given confidence level but doesn’t provide insights into the extremeness of potential further losses.","Worst-case scenario (WCS) offers detailed insights into extreme losses beyond what VaR provides, useful for robust risk management."]}]);
